<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahmad al-Mansur</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1549">1549</a>–<a href="/years/1603">1603</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahmad al-Mansur was the Saadi sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> from 1578 to 1603, enriched by his victory over the Portuguese at Alcácer Quibir. Seeking control of West African gold and salt, he sent an army equipped with firearms across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>, which defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tondibi">Battle of Tondibi</a> in 1591. The invasion led to the collapse of Songhai.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-mansur.jpg" alt="Ahmad al-Mansur" width="320" height="392" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: André Thevet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ahmad_al-Mansur_by_Andr%C3%A9_Thevet.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ahmad al-Mansur, also called al-Dhahabi, the Golden, was the fifth son of Mohammed ash-Sheikh, the first Saadi sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, and of Lalla Masuda. After his father was murdered in 1557 he and his brother Abd al-Malik fled their elder brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdallah-al-ghalib">Abdallah al-Ghalib</a> and stayed abroad until 1576. They spent seventeen years among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, between the Regency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. Ahmad received an extensive education in religious and secular sciences, among them theology, law, poetry, geometry, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">algebra</a> and astronomy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1578 his brother Abd al-Malik I died in battle against the Portuguese at Ksar el-Kebir, and Ahmad succeeded him with the prestige and the ransom money of the Portuguese captives. He used the ransom to fill the royal coffers and began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-badi-palace">El Badi Palace</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a> in December 1578; it was finished in 1593 or 1594. Eventually the treasury ran low under the cost of the army, spy services, building projects and a propaganda campaign for his claim to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>. He sent an ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I of England</a> in 1600 to negotiate an alliance against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><p>He recognised Ottoman suzerainty at the start of his reign but later had coins minted and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khutba">Friday prayers</a> said in his own name. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-iii">Murad III</a> prepared an attack, which was called off when al-Mansur sent an embassy with gifts and tribute. After 1587 he stopped his payments and proclaimed himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>. In 1583 and 1584 his forces annexed Saharan oases and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinguetti">Chinguetti</a>. On 16 October 1590 he dispatched an army of 4,000 men under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judar-pasha">Judar Pasha</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tondibi">Tondibi</a> it met a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a> force of 40,000 that lacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> weapons and quickly fled, and the army sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djenne">Djenné</a> and Gao.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Al-Mansur died in 1603 and was buried in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saadian-tombs">Saadian Tombs</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a>. His sons Zidan al-Nasir, in Marrakesh, and Abou Fares Abdallah, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a>, succeeded him. Holding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a> lands across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> proved too difficult, and the Saadians lost control of the cities not long after 1620. He balanced the Europeans and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> against each other to preserve Moroccan independence, though he spent far more than he collected. In 1602 he used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatwa">fatwas</a> to discourage the use of tobacco.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ahmad al-Mansur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the conquest of Songhai in 1591, gold from the Niger valley arrived in Marrakesh, earning <b>Ahmad al-Mansur</b> the name al-Dhahabi, the Golden, though the flow fell short of his hopes within a few years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saadi-sultanate">Saadi Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sa'adian sultan <b>Ahmad al-Mansur</b> sent an expeditionary force of around 4,000 men across the Sahara, armed with arquebuses and light cannon that the Songhai cavalry and infantry, however numerous, could not withstand.</blockquote><a href="/empire/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Fez, in what is now <a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a>. Died in Fez, in what is now <a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.04,-5&amp;year=1584" aria-label="Fez on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ahmad al-Mansur"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4646 4647h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4646 4647h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.04,-5&amp;year=1584">What was here in 1584? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
